          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter for, and when should a code from it be used instead of a disease code?",
          "answer": "The ICD-10-CM Chapter 18 covers \"Symptoms, signs, and abnormal findings\" and includes codes ranging from R00 to R99. This chapter categorizes various symptoms and signs, including abnormal findings from laboratory tests or imaging. For example, it includes symptoms like fever, headache, and abnormal findings in urine or blood, which may not be linked to a specific diagnosis at the time of coding (source: \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings\").\n\nCodes from this chapter are used instead of disease codes when there is an observation of a symptom or abnormal finding without a definitive diagnosis. For instance, if a patient presents with chest pain without a corresponding heart condition diagnosis, the symptom may be coded from Chapter 18. Additionally, areas like abnormal glucose findings without a diabetes diagnosis also belong in this chapter (source: \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings\").\n\nWhat I could not cover includes specific examples of how these codes are implemented in practice or any detailed procedural guidelines for coding beyond what is mentioned.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
